# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug
target

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

# Generated by cargo mutants
# Contains mutation testing data
**/mutants.out*/

# RustRover
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.  For a more nuclear
#  option (not recommended) you can uncomment the following to ignore the entire idea folder.
#.idea/

/dist/
/target/
# Cargo.lock is deliberately NOT ignored: `ido` ships as an application, not a
# library, so the lockfile is what makes a `vX.Y.Z` tag resolve to the same
# dependency graph in CI as it did locally. Committing it is what lets a release
# claim to correspond exactly to the tagged source.

# The Tauri sidecar binary staged by `just sidecar` (docs/mcp-server.md P3) —
# built per-machine per-target-triple, never committed.
/src-tauri/binaries/

# `dev-well/` itself IS committed: it's the fixture the `ido-dev` MCP server in
# .mcp.json.example is pinned to, so a clone that didn't have it would fail to
# start that server. Only the rebuildable per-machine cache is ignored — written
# if the fixture is ever opened in the app, and pure churn in a diff.
/dev-well/.ido/session.toml
/dev-well/assets/

.claude

# MCP client config: machine-local, never committed. `ido-dev` is portable
# (relative `dev-well/`) but `ido-docs` points at whatever folder *this* developer
# keeps their docs well in, so the whole file is per-machine. Copy
# `.mcp.json.example` to `.mcp.json` after cloning and fix up the paths.
/.mcp.json
